 ## Background
-The technique originated as a method to replace the brute-force approach of instantiating models of cubes in voxel engines [S1, unofficial]. By reconstructing cubes from triangles and checking for solid neighbors, engines could avoid rendering internal polygons, significantly improving performance [S1, unofficial]. This optimization was notably implemented in early voxel-based games like Minecraft, where meshes of all cubes were combined into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' to further optimize implementation, although this introduced restrictions such as vertex count limits (e.g., ~65,000 vertices in Unity) [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of removing interior polygons to improve rendering performance originated in early voxel engine implementations, such as those described in procedural terrain generation literature [S1, unofficial]. The initial optimization involved replacing the brute-force approach of instantiating models of cubes with a class that creates cubes from triangles, allowing each cube to check for solid neighbors and determine which sides to render [S1, unofficial]. This led to the combination of cube meshes into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' to further optimize implementation, although this introduced restrictions such as vertex count limits (e.g., ~65000 vertices in Unity) [S1, unofficial].
 
